{
  "requirement": "Before presenting, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to apply the revised text the reviews return. Where reviews conflict, for example two reviews that split the same requirement differently, it MUST direct them to reconcile the conflict, and to present as a design question any conflict whose resolution changes what is promised.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The requirement says the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to apply the revised text the reviews return. It also says the skill MUST direct them to reconcile conflicting reviews, and to present as a design question any conflict whose resolution changes what is promised. Every clause describes what the skill's instructions say. None names a library, algorithm, signature, type, field, or interface the skill has to be built with.\n\nNo alternative construction would keep the same instructions and still violate a clause. The same instructions could sit in the workflow list or in a different section, and the requirement would be met either way. Where they sit is a drafting choice.\n\nThe only implementation root is `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md` (whole file). The outline I was shown had most of its prose elided, so I could not read the instruction text itself. That file is the artifact the requirement governs. Whether its wording meets the three directions is a behavioral and content question, which this assessment leaves to the behavioral criterion. No structural implementation evidence is missing.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement governs the text of the bundled `habeas-requirements` skill. It has three obligations. The single marked test, `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_applies_and_reconciles_reviews_before_presenting]`, covers all three.\n\n1. **Apply the revised text the reviews return, before presenting.** `step_of(SKILL, \"Apply the revised text the reviews return\")` panics unless a workflow step contains that phrase. The final assertion, `apply < step_of(SKILL, \"Present each group to the user\")`, requires that step to come before the presenting step. The skill's step 3 contains the phrase.\n2. **Reconcile conflicting reviews, such as two that split the same requirement differently.** `assert_text` on `step(SKILL, apply)` requires the phrase \"reconcile reviews that clash, such as two that split the same requirement differently\" in that same step. `assert_text` asserts `text.contains` on each phrase, and `step` reads the whole collapsed step, including its continuation lines. The skill's step 3 contains this phrase.\n3. **Present as a design question any conflict whose resolution changes what is promised.** The same `assert_text` call requires \"a clash whose resolution changes what is promised becomes a design question\". The skill's step 3 contains this phrase.\n\nThe helpers `step`, `step_of`, `assert_text` and `workflow_steps` fail loudly when a step or phrase is missing, so the assertions are enforced. I found no further observable obligation. The test is a text and ordering check, which is the only way to test a requirement about skill content.",
